Authentication: challenge-response via SET_FEATURE/GET_FEATURE on report ID 6. Steps:
1. Send feature report [0x06, 0x00, 8×0x00] → reset the authentication state machine
2. GET_FEATURE(6, 10) → [0x06, 0x00, challenge(8B LE)]
3. Send feature [0x06, 0x01, 8×0x00] (our "challenge"/acknowledgement)
4. GET_FEATURE(6, 10) → [0x06, 0x02, response(8B)] (the keyboard's response to our challenge — ignored)
5. Send feature [0x06, 0x03, bmd_kbd_auth(challenge) as LE64]
6. GET_FEATURE(6, 10) → [0x06, 0x04, timeout(le16), ...] status; timeout ≈ 600 (seconds); re-authenticate before timeout.

Algorithm: bmd_kbd_auth: two 8-entry 64-bit lookup tables (EVEN/ODD), MASK 0xa79a63f585d37bf0, rol8 rotation of the challenge word, bit 0x78 selects the table. Let me re-read the exact algorithm to describe it precisely. Lines 135–178 of bmd.py. What I saw was:

```python
def rol8(v):
    return ((v << 56) | (v >> 8)) & 0xffffffffffffffff

def rol8n(v, n):
    ...
def bmd_kbd_auth(challenge):
    AUTH_EVEN_TBL = [...]
    AUTH_ODD_TBL = [...]
    MASK = 0xa79a63f585d37bf0
    ...
    if (v & 1) == ((0x78 >> n) & 1):
        k = AUTH_EVEN_TBL[n]
    else:
